\textbf{Invariant.} Once a slot is resolved, every write between its defining store and the load has already been checked. The walk must stop at a possible clobber of any remaining slot; scanning past it could invent a value. For $k$ writes and $n$ bytes, a straightforward mask scan costs $O(kn)$. Optimizers normally cap both the width and the search distance.

\subsection{Automatic Parallelization}\label{sec:alias-parallelization}

Alias analysis is essential for determining whether loop iterations can execute in parallel:

\begin{definition}[Parallelization Condition]\label{def:parallel-cond}
A loop can be parallelized if alias analysis proves that no two iterations access the same memory location with at least one write. Formally, for all pairs of memory accesses $a_1$ in iteration $i$ and $a_2$ in iteration $j$ ($i \neq j$): if $a_1$ is a write or $a_2$ is a write, then $a_1$ and $a_2$ are \textsc{NoAlias}.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Memory Disambiguation in Superscalar Processors}\label{sec:mem-disambiguation}

Hardware uses alias analysis (called \emph{memory disambiguation}) to determine whether loads and stores can be executed out of order:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Store-to-load forwarding}: If a load follows a store to the same address, the processor forwards the stored value directly.
  \item \textbf{Store-set prediction}: Predicts that a store will not alias with a subsequent load, allowing speculative execution.
  \item \textbf{Alias prediction buffers}: Hardware structures that cache alias analysis results for frequently executed load-store pairs.
\end{itemize}

\section{Alias Analysis in the Polyhedral Model}\label{sec:alias-polyhedral}

In the polyhedral model, alias analysis is performed using \emph{access relations} and \emph{dependence analysis}:

\begin{definition}[Polyhedral Alias Test]\label{def:poly-alias}
Two array accesses $A[f_1(\vec{i})]$ and $A[f_2(\vec{j})]$ may alias if and only if there exist iteration vectors $\vec{i}$ and $\vec{j}$ such that $f_1(\vec{i}) = f_2(\vec{j})$. This is checked by solving an integer linear program.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Polyhedral Alias]\label{ex:poly-alias}
For accesses $A[i]$ and $A[2*j + 1]$ in the iteration domain $\{i : 0 \leq i < N\}$, $\{j : 0 \leq j < N\}$:
\[
i = 2j + 1 \implies \text{aliases exist when } i \text{ is odd}
\]
The alias test confirms that accesses overlap for $i \in \{1, 3, 5, \ldots\}$.
\end{example}
